-- title:
--   Theorem A.1 — universal quantum inductive inference needs $\Omega(s/(\epsilon^2\delta))$ rounds
-- statement:
--   Let $c_0$ be a constant. There are constants $C>0$ and $c>0$ with the following property. Take any source model that hardwires biased coins with constant $c_0$, any learner $L$ for universal quantum inductive inference (Definition 4.1, computationally unbounded), and $\epsilon=1/a$, $\delta=1/b$ and $s$ with
--   $$0<\epsilon\le\tfrac1{64},\qquad 0<\delta\le\tfrac18,\qquad s\ \ge\ C\log_2\frac{2}{\epsilon\delta}.$$
--   Then there are a time bound $t$, a number of rounds
--   $$m\ \ge\ c\cdot\frac{s}{\epsilon^2\delta},$$
--   a program $T$ with $|T|\le s$ and computational-basis instruments $\mathcal I_1,\dots,\mathcal I_m$ on one-qubit registers ($n=1$), such that $L$ fails on the source generated by $T$:
--   $$\Pr_{\substack{i\leftarrow[m]\\ x_{<i}\leftarrow D^{\mathcal I,\rho}_{<i}}}\Big[\mathrm{TD}\big(L(\mathsf{param},h_{<i},\mathcal I_i,\rho_{<i|h_{<i}}),\ \tau_{\le i|h_{<i}}\big)\le\epsilon\Big]\ <\ 1-\delta .$$
--
--   Consequently every valid round complexity exceeds $c\,s/(\epsilon^2\delta)$, so the $O(s\epsilon^{-2}\delta^{-1})$ bound of Theorem 4.2 is optimal up to a constant factor, even for computationally unbounded learners, $n=1$, product sources and computational-basis instruments.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the paper's source model (a classical Turing machine hardwiring α outputs a circuit generating independent biased bits, Eqs. (186)–(188), p.38) → every SourceModel with HardwiresBiasedCoins c₀; C and c depend only on c₀; reason: this is the property of the paper's model that the proof uses, and the paper shows its model has it (p.38); a lower bound over an arbitrary source model is false
--   - encoding: requires Ω(s/(ε²δ)) rounds: any valid round complexity satisfies r(s, ε⁻¹, δ⁻¹) > m = Ω(s/(ε²δ)) → ∃ c > 0, and for each admissible (a, b, s) ∃ t, m ≥ c·s·a²·b, T with |T| ≤ s and computational-basis instruments with successProb < 1 − 1/b; reason: failure at such an m is exactly what forces r > m (Eq. (192))
--   - encoding: computationally unbounded learners; computational-basis instruments; product states with n = 1 → the base Learner (families of channels); instruments with branches ρ ↦ |x⟩⟨x|ρ|x⟩⟨x|, x ∈ {0,1}; gen 1 t T a product of one-qubit diagonal states (from the hypothesis); reason: Definition 4.1 of the base mission; same instances
--   - encoding: ε⁻¹, δ⁻¹ ∈ ℕ with 0 < ε ≤ 1/64, 0 < δ ≤ 1/8; log₂ → a, b ∈ ℕ with a ≥ 64, b ≥ 8, ε = 1/a, δ = 1/b; Real.logb 2; reason: Lean convention; same instances
-- source:
--   Hiroka, Hsieh, Shirakawa, Universal Inductive Inference of Quantum States, arXiv:2609.36912 (PDF of 2026-11-02, sha256 ac6387756651cc50c671f36acf1ef987db0df0c463581cd948250204441f6925), Appendix A, Theorem A.1, p.35, proof pp.35–38

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QIndInf_successProb
import Definitions.Def_QIndInf_Supplement_HardwiresBiasedCoins

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace QIndInf.Supplement

open QIndInf QAlgorithms.CopyMeasurements

/-- **Theorem A.1 (lower bound `Ω(s/(ε²δ))` on the round complexity).** For every source model with
the hardwiring property of the paper's encoding (constant `c₀`) there are constants `C > 0` and
`c > 0` such that for every learner `L` (computationally unbounded, Definition 4.1) and all
`ε = 1/a`, `δ = 1/b` and `s` with `ε ≤ 1/64`, `δ ≤ 1/8` and `s ≥ C log₂(2/(εδ))`, there are a time
bound `t`, a number of rounds `m ≥ c · s/(ε²δ)`, a program `T` with `|T| ≤ s` generating a product
state on one-qubit registers (`n = 1`), and computational-basis instruments `I₁, …, I_m`, for which
`L` fails: its success probability is `< 1 − δ`. Hence every valid round complexity
`r(s, ε⁻¹, δ⁻¹)` exceeds `c · s/(ε²δ)`.

arXiv:2609.36912 (PDF of 2026-11-02), Appendix A, Theorem A.1, p.35, proof pp.35–38.

**Formalization Note.**
* The learner is the base mission's `Learner` (a family of channels, no efficiency) and the success
  probability is the base mission's `successProb` (Definition 4.1, Eq. (56)).
* A lower bound over an arbitrary source model is false (a model whose short programs generate
  nothing hard makes inference easy), so the source model is required to have the property the
  proof uses (`HardwiresBiasedCoins c₀`, Eqs. (186)–(188)); `C` and `c` depend only on `c₀`.
* `T` generates a product state because the hypothesis gives `gen 1 t T = coinState …`, a product
  of one-qubit diagonal states.
* The computational-basis instrument has the branches `ρ ↦ |x⟩⟨x| ρ |x⟩⟨x|`, `x ∈ {0,1}`.
* `log₂` is `Real.logb 2`. -/
theorem theorem_A_1 :
    ∀ c₀ : ℝ, ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧ ∃ c : ℝ, 0 < c ∧
      ∀ gen : SourceModel, HardwiresBiasedCoins c₀ gen →
        ∀ L : Learner, ∀ a b s : ℕ, 64 ≤ a → 8 ≤ b →
          C * Real.logb 2 (2 * (a : ℝ) * b) ≤ s →
          ∃ t m : ℕ, c * s * (a : ℝ) ^ 2 * b ≤ m ∧
            ∃ T : List Bool, T.length ≤ s ∧
              ∃ I : (j : Fin m) → QuantumInstrument (Reg 1) (Fin 2),
                (∀ (j : Fin m) (x : Fin 2) (ρ : Matrix (Reg 1) (Reg 1) ℂ),
                  instrumentBranch (I j) x ρ =
                    (Matrix.single x x 1 : Matrix (Reg 1) (Reg 1) ℂ) * ρ *
                      (Matrix.single x x 1 : Matrix (Reg 1) (Reg 1) ℂ)) ∧
                successProb L s t a b (gen 1 t T) (fun _ => 2) I < 1 - 1 / (b : ℝ) := by sorry

end QIndInf.Supplement
